<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=gunpowder">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Weapons and war</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gunpowder</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gunpowder is an explosive mixture of saltpetre, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charcoal">charcoal</a> and sulphur, first developed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. Its use in weapons spread under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> and Yuan dynasties and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ming-empire">Ming Empire</a>, and later across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>. It served in the armies of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/majapahit-empire">Majapahit Empire</a>, and armies equipped with it overcame states that lacked it, such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/songhai-empire">Songhai Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gunpowder.jpg" alt="Gunpowder" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Lord Mountbatten · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Black_Powder-1.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…on 21 April 1526, where Ibrahim Lodi's much larger army, reportedly some 100,000 strong, was destroyed by Babur's combination of field guns and matchlocks behind a barrier of linked carts and the flanking tulughma manoeuvre — among the first decisive uses of <b>gunpowder</b> artillery in north Indian warfare.</blockquote><a href="/empire/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Later Goryeo supplied ships and troops for the failed Yuan invasions of Japan in 1274 and 1281, and late in the dynasty Choe Mu-seon's <b>gunpowder</b> office armed ships with cannon that destroyed a Japanese pirate fleet at Jinpo in 1380.</blockquote><a href="/empire/goryeo">Goryeo · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Jin armies repelled a Song offensive in 1206–1208, but from 1211 the Mongols proved irresistible, destroying Jin field armies at Yehuling and, in 1232, at Sanfengshan, despite a stubborn defence of walled cities with <b>gunpowder</b> weapons such as the iron 'thunder-crash' bombs used at Kaifeng.</blockquote><a href="/empire/jin-dynasty-1115-1234">Jin dynasty (1115-1234) · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>At the fall of Kansala in 1867, the mansaba Janke Waali had the <b>gunpowder</b> stores ignited, destroying the city, its defenders and many of the attackers.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kaabu-empire">Kaabu Empire · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Infantry used crossbows, pikes and <b>gunpowder</b> weapons including cannon and firearms such as hand cannons, while cavalry provided mobility and rapid response.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ming-empire">Ming Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>…securing the overland routes under the Pax Mongolica, it allowed goods, ideas and people to travel between China, the Middle East and Europe, from merchants such as Marco Polo to envoys such as the Franciscan William of Rubruck, and fostered exchanges in astronomy, medicine, cartography and <b>gunpowder</b> technology.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mongol-empire">Mongol Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
